# English articles, in display order. `seo` is the <title> text (60 characters or fewer).
ARTS = [
dict(slug='outgrowing-spreadsheets-and-accounting-software',
 tag='Operations',
 title='Seven signs you have outgrown spreadsheets and accounting software',
 seo='Signs you have outgrown spreadsheets and accounting software',
 desc='How to tell when spreadsheets and entry-level accounting software are holding the business back, and what to move into an ERP first.',
 short='You have outgrown spreadsheets when the same number lives in three places, month-end takes longer every quarter and only one person understands the workbook. Move inventory and purchasing into one system with accounting first, then sales.',
 faq=[('When should a company move to an ERP?', 'When the cost of keeping separate tools in step is visible: data typed more than once, stock figures nobody trusts, a slow month-end and decisions waiting on someone to build a report. Revenue is a rough guide, and the signs matter more than the number.'),
      ('Can we keep our accounting software and add separate inventory tools?', 'You can, but every link between tools is something to maintain, and the numbers drift whenever a sync fails or someone skips a step. One system removes the links.'),
      ('What data do we need ready before moving to an ERP?', 'A clean item list with units and costs, your customer and supplier lists, open orders and bills, stock on hand by location, and opening balances from your accountant.')],
 body='''
<p>Spreadsheets and an entry-level accounting package are the right tools for a young company. They are cheap and everyone knows them. The trouble starts quietly, somewhere after the first few million in revenue, when the business has more moving parts than the tools were built for. These are the signs.</p>
<h2>1. The same data is typed more than once</h2>
<p>An order is written in an email, entered on a sheet, then keyed into the accounting package as an invoice. Each step is a chance for a wrong quantity or price, and each one costs minutes that add up to days.</p>
<h2>2. Nobody trusts the stock number</h2>
<p>Sales asks the warehouse before promising anything, because the sheet is usually wrong. Staff walk the shelves to find out what to buy. When the record and the shelf disagree, people stop using the record.</p>
<h2>3. Month-end takes longer every quarter</h2>
<p>Closing the books means collecting exports, matching them by hand and chasing differences. If the close has grown from days to weeks, the tools are no longer keeping up with the volume.</p>
<h2>4. One person holds the workbook together</h2>
<p>There is a master spreadsheet with formulas only its author understands. When that person is on holiday, questions wait. When they leave, the company loses part of its memory.</p>
<h2>5. Simple questions take days</h2>
<p>What is our margin on this customer? What is on order and when does it land? Which bills are overdue? If each answer needs someone to build a report, decisions are being made late or on instinct.</p>
<h2>6. Adding people means sharing logins</h2>
<p>Per-user pricing pushes teams to share accounts, so nobody can tell who changed what. A system that charges per seat discourages exactly the people on the floor who should be recording the work.</p>
<h2>7. Customers notice</h2>
<p>A wrong price on an invoice, a short shipment or a late order all trace back to information that did not reach the right person in time. By the time customers see the cracks, the team has been working around them for months.</p>
<h2>What changes with one system</h2>
<p>In an ERP, a purchase order, the goods received, the supplier bill and the accounting entry are one chain. A sale reduces stock and posts to the books without anyone re-keying it. Each role sees the same live numbers.</p>
<h2>What to move first</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Inventory and purchasing,</strong> because that is where most double entry and most drift come from.</li><li><strong>Accounting,</strong> so stock movements and bills post to the books as they happen.</li><li><strong>Sales,</strong> so orders and customer prices use the same records.</li></ol>
<h2>Prepare before you switch</h2>
<p>Clean your item list, agree on units and costs, tidy the customer and supplier lists, and ask your accountant for opening balances. A system loaded with clean data on day one earns the team's trust quickly. You do not need everything perfect: start with the records you use every day.</p>'''),
dict(slug='how-to-choose-an-erp',
 tag='Buying guide',
 title='How to choose an ERP for a growing business',
 seo='How to choose an ERP for a growing business',
 desc='A practical checklist for companies comparing ERP systems: what to test live, what to ask about your data and hosting, and what to ignore in the demo.',
 short='Pick the ERP that completes your own ten everyday tasks live with your data, keeps inventory, purchasing, sales and accounting in one record, runs on an instance that is yours, and has a price that does not rise with headcount.',
 faq=[('What should a growing business look for in an ERP?', 'Look for one system where a sale, the stock movement and the accounting entry are the same record. Add screens built for each role, warehouse screens that work on a phone, your own instance with full data export, and a price that does not grow with every new user.'),
      ('How do I compare ERP vendors fairly?', 'Write down your ten most common tasks, ask each vendor to do them live with your sample data, and score each task pass or fail. Ignore everything else in the demo.'),
      ('How long does an ERP implementation take?', 'It depends on how many processes and records you bring across. A setup that starts from an industry preset is usually measured in weeks. Ask each vendor for a dated plan before you sign.')],
 body='''
<p>Most companies choose an ERP the way they chose their first accounting package: a polished demo, a price per user and a gut feeling. A year later the warehouse still runs on a spreadsheet and finance re-types the numbers. This checklist is for owners and operations leads who want to avoid that.</p>
<h2>Start with how the business actually runs</h2>
<p>Write down what your team does every day. A distributor takes orders, buys from suppliers, receives pallets and invoices customers at agreed prices. A retailer sells, restocks and counts. The system has to fit those moves. If a demo spends ten minutes on features you will never use, that tells you who the product was built for.</p>
<h2>Insist on one record, not three</h2>
<p>The most common failure is three tools joined by exports: orders in one place, stock in a spreadsheet, the books somewhere else. Ask one question in every demo: <strong>when we ship an order, what changes, and where?</strong> A good answer is that stock drops, the invoice posts to the books and the customer balance updates, with nobody typing it twice.</p>
<h2>Test it where the work happens</h2>
<p>Receiving, transfers and counts happen on the warehouse floor. Open the product on a phone and receive a delivery against a purchase order. If you have to pinch and zoom to press a button, your staff will go back to paper.</p>
<h2>Ask where your data lives</h2>
<ul><li>Do you get your own instance, or do you share one system with every other customer?</li><li>Can you export all of your records, in a normal format, at any time?</li><li>Who at the vendor can see your data, and why?</li><li>What does it cost, in time and money, to bring your existing items, customers and balances in?</li></ul>
<h2>Look at the pricing model, not only the price</h2>
<p>A low price per user gets expensive once sales, warehouse staff and a bookkeeper all need access. Shared logins are the usual workaround, and they destroy your audit trail. Ask whether users are unlimited, whether setup is a one-time fee, and whether hosting and updates are included.</p>
<h2>Check who sets it up</h2>
<p>Software is half of an ERP project. The other half is configuration, data migration and getting the chart of accounts right. Ask who does that work, whether they understand accounting, and what happens after go-live when something needs to change.</p>
<h2>Be honest about what is finished</h2>
<p>Ask which features clients use today and which are still being built. A reputable vendor will tell you plainly. Anything labelled "coming soon" should not be part of the decision.</p>
<h2>A simple scoring method</h2>
<ol><li>List your must-have tasks, no more than ten.</li><li>Have each vendor perform each task live, with your own sample data.</li><li>Score each one pass or fail.</li></ol>
<p>The system that passes the most real tasks, on an instance you control, with a price you can predict, is usually the right one.</p>'''),
dict(slug='private-cloud-erp-vs-saas-vs-on-premise',
 tag='Buying guide',
 title='Shared SaaS, private cloud or on-premise: where should your ERP live?',
 seo='SaaS vs private cloud vs on-premise ERP',
 desc='A plain comparison of the three ways to host an ERP, and what each one means for control of your operations data, upkeep and cost.',
 short='Shared SaaS is the quickest to start but puts your data in a system shared with every other customer. On-premise gives you full control and all of the upkeep. A private cloud instance sits between them: your own system and data, with the vendor carrying the hosting.',
 faq=[('What is the difference between shared SaaS and a private cloud ERP?', 'In shared, or multi-tenant, SaaS every customer uses the same running system and the vendor separates the data inside it. In a private cloud setup each customer has its own instance. You get more separation and more room to configure, while the vendor still handles hosting and updates.'),
      ('Is an on-premise ERP more secure than a cloud ERP?', 'Not automatically. Security depends on patching, backups and access control, and all of them take staff time. On-premise gives you physical control and also makes you responsible for every one of those jobs.'),
      ('What should I ask an ERP vendor about my data?', 'Ask whether you get your own instance, whether you can export everything at any time, who at the vendor can access your data, how often backups run and where they are kept, and what happens to your data if you leave.')],
 body='''
<p>An ERP holds the numbers a company least wants to leak or lose: customer prices, supplier costs, margins and the books. Where that system runs decides who looks after it and who can reach it. There are three models, and the differences are practical.</p>
<h2>The three models</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Shared SaaS.</strong> Every customer signs in to the same running system. The vendor keeps each company's data apart inside it. It is quick to start and the vendor does all the upkeep, but you get the product as it is, on the vendor's schedule.</li><li><strong>On-premise.</strong> You buy or rent a server, install the software and maintain it. The data never leaves your building, and every patch, backup and failure is yours to handle.</li><li><strong>Private cloud.</strong> You get your own instance, hosted by the vendor. The system and its data are yours alone, and the vendor still carries hosting and updates.</li></ul>
<h2>What "kept internal" really means</h2>
<p>Owners who say they want operations kept internal usually mean three things: nobody outside the company should see the data, the system should bend to how the company works, and the company should be able to leave with everything it put in. Judge each model against those three, not against the word "cloud".</p>
<h2>Compare the real costs</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Hardware:</strong> servers, networking and replacement after failures, for on-premise only.</li><li><strong>Time:</strong> updates, security patches and restoring from backups.</li><li><strong>Downtime:</strong> who gets called when it stops on a Saturday.</li><li><strong>Growth:</strong> what a second location or ten more users adds to the bill.</li></ul>
<h2>Where each one fits</h2>
<p>Shared SaaS suits companies with standard processes that are happy to adapt to the product. On-premise suits companies with IT staff and a rule or contract that keeps data on site. A private instance suits companies that want their own system, configured to their operations, without running servers.</p>
<h2>Questions to ask any vendor</h2>
<ol><li>Do we get our own instance, or a place in a shared one?</li><li>Can we export all of our data whenever we want?</li><li>Who on your side can access our data, and is that access recorded?</li><li>How often are backups taken and where are they kept?</li><li>What happens to our data if we stop being a customer?</li></ol>
<h2>Our view</h2>
<p>Nastaliq is private cloud only: every client, whatever its size, runs on its own dedicated server with its own branded site. We handle setup and hosting, so your team spends its time on operations instead of servers. We do not sell on-premise installs. If a contract requires your data to stay inside your building, we are not the right fit, and we will say so.</p>'''),
dict(slug='why-inventory-counts-drift',
 tag='Inventory',
 title='Why inventory counts drift, and how barcode scanning fixes it',
 seo='Why inventory counts drift and how to fix it',
 desc='The usual reasons your stock numbers stop matching the shelf, and the small habits and tools that close the gap.',
 short='Inventory counts drift because stock moves or arrives without being recorded at the moment it happens. Scanning barcodes at receiving, transfer and sale, plus small weekly counts, keeps the system and the shelf in step.',
 faq=[('Why does my inventory never match the shelf?', 'Usually because receiving, transfers, returns or damage were recorded late, wrongly or not at all. The longer the gap between the event and the entry, the bigger the drift.'),
      ('Does barcode scanning really improve stock accuracy?', 'Yes. Scanning identifies the exact item and records it at the moment it moves, which removes most typing mistakes and forgotten entries.'),
      ('How often should a warehouse count stock?', 'Count a small group of fast-moving items every week instead of the whole warehouse once a year. Problems show up sooner and are easier to trace.')],
 body='''
<p>You count the shelf on Monday and the system agrees. By Friday it doesn't. Inventory drift is normal, and it almost always comes from the same handful of causes.</p>
<h2>Where the numbers go wrong</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Receiving errors.</strong> A delivery is checked against the packing slip, but the slip is wrong, or the count is entered later from memory.</li><li><strong>Unrecorded movement.</strong> Stock moves between rooms, trucks or locations without a transfer being entered.</li><li><strong>Typing mistakes.</strong> One extra zero on a quantity is enough to hide a shortage for weeks.</li><li><strong>Returns and damage.</strong> Items are thrown away or returned to suppliers with no adjustment.</li><li><strong>Substitutions at the counter.</strong> A customer gets a different size than the one rung up.</li></ul>
<h2>Why delay makes it worse</h2>
<p>The longer the gap between an event and recording it, the more likely it is to be forgotten or entered wrong. Paper notes that get typed in at the end of the day are the biggest source of drift in small warehouses and shops.</p>
<h2>What barcode scanning changes</h2>
<p>Scanning replaces typing with a camera or scanner. The item is identified by its code, so you can't mistype a name or pick the wrong variant. When scanning happens at the moment of receiving, transferring or selling, the system is updated while the stock is still in your hands.</p>
<h2>Habits that keep counts honest</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Receive against the purchase order,</strong> not against memory.</li><li><strong>Record every transfer</strong> between locations, even short trips.</li><li><strong>Count a little every week.</strong> Cycle counts of a few fast-moving items catch problems sooner than one big count a year.</li><li><strong>Investigate differences</strong> once, then fix the cause, not just the number.</li><li><strong>Give staff phone-sized screens</strong> so recording takes seconds.</li></ol>
<h2>Set a reorder level for important items</h2>
<p>When stock is accurate you can trust a reorder alert. A system that tells you six items are below their minimum each morning removes the need to walk the shelves just to find out what to buy.</p>
<h2>Start small</h2>
<p>You don't need to fix everything at once. Pick your twenty fastest-moving items, scan them in and out for a month and compare against a physical count. Most owners see the difference quickly.</p>'''),
dict(slug='bilingual-business-software-canada',
 tag='Canada',
 title='Bilingual ERP software for Canadian businesses',
 seo='Bilingual ERP software for Canadian businesses',
 desc='Why English and French support matters for invoices, receipts and staff screens, and what to check before you buy.',
 short="Good bilingual software lets each user pick a language and prints invoices and receipts in the customer's language, with Canadian French terms. Test it with your own documents before you buy, and check provincial rules with an adviser.",
 faq=[('Does business software need to support French in Canada?', 'It depends on your province, customers and business type. Many Canadian businesses serve both languages, so a system that switches cleanly saves retyping. Confirm your own obligations with an accountant or adviser.'),
      ('What should I check in bilingual business software?', 'Check that each user can choose a language, that invoices and receipts can print in either language, and that emails, reports and error messages are translated, not only the menu.'),
      ('Is Canadian French different from European French in software?', 'Yes. Some business terms differ, for example courriel for email and téléverser for upload. Ask whether the translation was written for Canadian users.')],
 body='''
<p>If you sell in Canada, some of your customers will prefer French, and some of your staff will too. Software that only works well in English creates friction on both sides of the counter.</p>
<h2>Where language shows up</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Customer documents:</strong> invoices, quotes, receipts and statements.</li><li><strong>Staff screens:</strong> menus, buttons, error messages and reports.</li><li><strong>Emails and notifications</strong> that your system sends on your behalf.</li><li><strong>Product names and descriptions</strong> you enter yourself.</li></ul>
<h2>Translation is not the same as Canadian French</h2>
<p>Business vocabulary differs between France and Canada. Terms for receipts, taxes and payment methods are not always the same, and staff notice when a screen sounds foreign. Ask the vendor whether the French is written for Canadian users, and have a French-speaking colleague click through a real invoice.</p>
<h2>Questions to ask a vendor</h2>
<ol><li>Can each user choose their own language, or is it one setting for the whole company?</li><li>Can one customer receive French documents while another receives English?</li><li>Are reports, emails and error messages translated, or only the main menu?</li><li>Who maintains the translations when new features are added?</li></ol>
<h2>Check your own obligations</h2>
<p>Language requirements for commercial documents and signage differ by province and by business type. Speak to your accountant or a qualified adviser about what applies to you, rather than relying on a software vendor's summary.</p>
<h2>The practical benefit</h2>
<p>A bilingual system lets you hire from a wider pool, serve more customers and hand documents to either side without retyping. For a growing business that sells across provincial lines, that matters more each year.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>Before you buy, ask for a demo in French and in English using your own product names. If the system handles both cleanly, you have one less thing to worry about later.</p>'''),
]
